Lab 18: Monitor VM Metrics with Prometheus and Grafana
Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Enable and verify the OpenShift user workload monitoring stack
Deploy a virtual machine with OpenShift Virtualization and confirm it is exposing metrics
Configure a ServiceMonitor and PrometheusRule to collect and alert on VM metrics
Query VM performance data directly from Prometheus and interpret the results
Troubleshoot common gaps in a VM monitoring pipeline
Prerequisites
Before starting this lab, you should have:

Basic understanding of Kubernetes and OpenShift concepts (namespaces, pods, services)
Familiarity with YAML configuration files
Basic knowledge of virtual machine concepts (CPU, memory, disk, network)
Experience with command-line interfaces
Access to an OpenShift cluster with cluster-admin rights and the OpenShift Virtualization (CNV) operator already installed
Required tools (verify they are installed and on your PATH):

oc version --client
No additional client-side packages need to be installed for this lab; all components are deployed into the cluster using oc apply.

