Task 1: Enable Monitoring and Deploy a Monitored VM
Step 1.1: Verify the cluster and existing monitoring stack
# Verify cluster connection
oc whoami
oc cluster-info

# Confirm the platform monitoring stack is running
oc get pods -n openshift-monitoring | grep prometheus-k8s

# Confirm OpenShift Virtualization is installed
oc get csv -n openshift-cnv | grep kubevirt-hyperconverged
Deliverable: Output showing at least one prometheus-k8s-* pod in Running state, and a kubevirt-hyperconverged-operator CSV with phase Succeeded.

Step 1.2: Enable user workload monitoring
By default, OpenShift only monitors platform components. To scrape metrics from workloads in your own namespaces (including VM launcher pods), you must enable user workload monitoring.

cat << 'EOF' | oc apply -f -
apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: cluster-monitoring-config
  namespace: openshift-monitoring
data:
  config.yaml: |
    enableUserWorkload: true
EOF
Wait for the user workload monitoring components to start:

oc get pods -n openshift-user-workload-monitoring -w
Press Ctrl+C once you see prometheus-user-workload-0 and prometheus-user-workload-1 in Running state, along with a thanos-ruler-user-workload-0 pod.

Deliverable: oc get pods -n openshift-user-workload-monitoring shows the prometheus-user-workload-* and thanos-ruler-user-workload-* pods as Running.

Step 1.3: Create a namespace and deploy a monitored VM
oc new-project vm-workloads
oc label namespace vm-workloads openshift.io/cluster-monitoring=true
The openshift.io/cluster-monitoring=true label is what tells the user workload Prometheus that ServiceMonitors in this namespace should be scraped.

Deploy a small test VM:

cat << 'EOF' | oc apply -f -
apiVersion: kubevirt.io/v1
kind: VirtualMachine
metadata:
  name: web-server-vm
  namespace: vm-workloads
  labels:
    app: web-server
spec:
  running: true
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        kubevirt.io/vm: web-server-vm
    spec:
      domain:
        cpu:
          cores: 1
        devices:
          disks:
          - disk:
              bus: virtio
            name: containerdisk
          - disk:
              bus: virtio
            name: cloudinitdisk
          interfaces:
          - masquerade: {}
            name: default
        resources:
          requests:
            memory: 1Gi
      networks:
      - name: default
        pod: {}
      volumes:
      - containerDisk:
          image: quay.io/kubevirt/fedora-cloud-container-disk-demo
        name: containerdisk
      - cloudInitNoCloud:
          userData: |
            #cloud-config
            password: fedora
            chpasswd: { expire: False }
        name: cloudinitdisk
EOF
Verify the VM starts:

oc get vm -n vm-workloads
oc get vmi -n vm-workloads
oc get pods -n vm-workloads
Deliverable: oc get vmi -n vm-workloads shows web-server-vm with PHASE equal to Running, and a corresponding virt-launcher-web-server-vm-* pod in Running state.

Task 2: Collect and Query VM Metrics
Step 2.1: Confirm KubeVirt is exposing metrics
KubeVirt components (virt-controller, virt-handler, virt-api) expose Prometheus metrics automatically once the HyperConverged operator is installed, and the operator ships its own ServiceMonitors in the openshift-cnv namespace. Confirm this:

oc get servicemonitor -n openshift-cnv
Deliverable: A list of ServiceMonitors such as kubevirt-prometheus-metrics or similar KubeVirt-managed ServiceMonitors in the openshift-cnv namespace. These are created automatically by the HyperConverged operator; you do not need to author your own ServiceMonitor for core KubeVirt metrics.

Step 2.2: Query VM metrics from Prometheus
OpenShift exposes Prometheus through a route. Use it to query metrics directly with curl.

# Get the Prometheus route host
PROM_HOST=$(oc get route prometheus-k8s -n openshift-monitoring -o jsonpath='{.spec.host}')

# Get a token for your current user (must have access to the thanos-querier/prometheus API)
TOKEN=$(oc whoami -t)

# Query for KubeVirt VMI CPU metrics (via thanos-querier, which aggregates
# both platform and user workload Prometheus data)
THANOS_HOST=$(oc get route thanos-querier -n openshift-monitoring -o jsonpath='{.spec.host}')

curl -sk -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \
  "https://$THANOS_HOST/api/v1/query?query=kubevirt_vmi_info" | head -c 1000
Deliverable: A JSON response with "status":"success" and a result array containing at least one time series with a name label equal to web-server-vm. If the array is empty, wait one to two minutes for the next scrape interval and retry.

Step 2.3: Query CPU and memory usage for the VM
THANOS_HOST=$(oc get route thanos-querier -n openshift-monitoring -o jsonpath='{.spec.host}')
TOKEN=$(oc whoami -t)

# CPU seconds consumed by the VM's vCPUs, rate over the last 5 minutes
curl -sk -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \
  --data-urlencode 'query=rate(kubevirt_vmi_vcpu_seconds_total{name="web-server-vm"}[5m])' \
  "https://$THANOS_HOST/api/v1/query"

# Memory currently used by the VM's guest OS, in bytes
curl -sk -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \
  --data-urlencode 'query=kubevirt_vmi_memory_available_bytes{name="web-server-vm"}' \
  "https://$THANOS_HOST/api/v1/query"
Deliverable: Both queries return "status":"success". The first may return an empty result array if the VM has been idle for the full 5-minute window (no CPU seconds accumulated yet); the second should return a numeric value for kubevirt_vmi_memory_available_bytes, confirming KubeVirt is reporting memory metrics for the VM.

Step 2.4: Create a PrometheusRule to alert on high VM CPU usage
cat << 'EOF' | oc apply -f -
apiVersion: monitoring.coreos.com/v1
kind: PrometheusRule
metadata:
  name: vm-alerts
  namespace: vm-workloads
  labels:
    role: alert-rules
spec:
  groups:
  - name: vm.rules
    rules:
    - alert: VMHighCPUUsage
      expr: sum(rate(kubevirt_vmi_vcpu_seconds_total{name="web-server-vm"}[5m])) by (name) > 0.8
      for: 5m
      labels:
        severity: warning
      annotations:
        summary: "VM {{ $labels.name }} has high CPU usage"
        description: "VM {{ $labels.name }} vCPU usage has averaged above 80% of one core for more than 5 minutes."
EOF
Verify the rule was accepted:

oc get prometheusrule vm-alerts -n vm-workloads
oc get prometheusrule vm-alerts -n vm-workloads -o jsonpath='{.spec.groups[0].rules[0].alert}'
Deliverable: oc get prometheusrule vm-alerts -n vm-workloads returns the object, and the jsonpath command prints VMHighCPUUsage, confirming the rule is syntactically valid and stored.

Task 3: Verify the Monitoring Pipeline End to End
Step 3.1: Generate CPU load inside the VM's launcher pod
POD_NAME=$(oc get pods -n vm-workloads -l kubevirt.io/vm=web-server-vm -o jsonpath='{.items[0].metadata.name}')
echo "$POD_NAME"

# Run a short CPU burn inside the compute container of the launcher pod
oc exec -n vm-workloads "$POD_NAME" -c compute -- /bin/sh -c "timeout 120 sh -c 'while true; do :; done' &"
If your cluster does not permit exec into the compute container, it is sufficient to let the VM run idle and observe baseline metrics in the next step; note this in your results.

Step 3.2: Re-query metrics and confirm the rule is loaded in Prometheus
THANOS_HOST=$(oc get route thanos-querier -n openshift-monitoring -o jsonpath='{.spec.host}')
TOKEN=$(oc whoami -t)

curl -sk -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \
  --data-urlencode 'query=rate(kubevirt_vmi_vcpu_seconds_total{name="web-server-vm"}[5m])' \
  "https://$THANOS_HOST/api/v1/query"
Then confirm the alerting rule has been picked up by the user workload Prometheus:

curl -sk -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \
  "https://$THANOS_HOST/api/v1/rules" | grep -o "VMHighCPUUsage"
Deliverable: The CPU query returns a non-empty result array with a value greater than 0 (confirming vCPU activity is being scraped), and the second command prints VMHighCPUUsage, confirming Prometheus has loaded your alert rule and is evaluating it.

Step 3.3: Check alert state
curl -sk -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \
  "https://$THANOS_HOST/api/v1/alerts" | grep -A5 "VMHighCPUUsage"
Deliverable: Either no output (the alert has not fired because the 5-minute for duration has not elapsed or the threshold was not crossed) or a JSON block showing "state":"pending" or "state":"firing" for VMHighCPUUsage. Both outcomes confirm the rule is being actively evaluated by Prometheus.

Expected Outcomes
A running virtual machine in the vm-workloads namespace with its metrics visible in the OpenShift monitoring stack
Verified curl-based queries against the Thanos Querier API returning real KubeVirt metric names (kubevirt_vmi_info, kubevirt_vmi_vcpu_seconds_total, kubevirt_vmi_memory_available_bytes)
A working PrometheusRule (VMHighCPUUsage) loaded and evaluated by the user workload Prometheus instance
Troubleshooting
Issue 1: Thanos Querier queries return an empty result array for kubevirt_vmi_info

Symptom: "status":"success" but "result":[]. Diagnostic hint: check that the vm-workloads namespace has the openshift.io/cluster-monitoring=true label (oc get namespace vm-workloads --show-labels), and confirm at least 60-90 seconds have passed since the VM entered the Running phase for the first scrape to complete.

Issue 2: curl to the Thanos Querier route returns 401 Unauthorized or 403 Forbidden

Symptom: HTTP error instead of JSON. Diagnostic hint: confirm your token is current with oc whoami -t (tokens can expire), and verify your user has the cluster-monitoring-view role bound, since anonymous or under-privileged users cannot query the monitoring API by default.

Conclusion
In this lab, you enabled OpenShift's user workload monitoring, deployed a virtual machine under OpenShift Virtualization, and confirmed that KubeVirt's built-in metrics pipeline exposes real CPU, memory, and identity metrics for that VM through Prometheus and the Thanos Querier API. You then authored and validated a PrometheusRule that alerts on sustained high vCPU usage, and verified end to end that the rule was loaded and actively evaluated by the cluster's monitoring stack. These are the same mechanisms used to monitor production virtual machine workloads on OpenShift, so the ServiceMonitor, PrometheusRule, and query patterns you practiced here translate directly to building dashboards and alerting policies for real VM fleets. As a next step, consider extending the alert rule set with memory and storage thresholds, and wiring Alertmanager routes to a real notification channel such as email or a chat webhook.
